2. TECHNOLOGY STACK
--------------------
Frontend Framework : React 18.3.1
Build Tool         : Vite 5.4.2
Language           : JavaScript (JSX), CSS3
Fonts              : Bebas Neue, IBM Plex Mono, IBM Plex Sans (Google Fonts)
Map Provider       : Custom SVG map (OpenStreetMap data, no API key required)
API Communication  : Fetch API (async/await)
Backend (Part 1)   : PHP 7+, MySQL, Apache (webdev.aut.ac.nz)
Deployment         : StackBlitz (Part 2), AUT webdev server (Part 1 API)


3. HOW TO RUN AND BUILD THE PROJECT LOCALLY
--------------------------------------------
Prerequisites:
  - Node.js 18+ installed
  - npm 9+ installed

Steps:
  1. Unzip Part2_hpr7339.zip
  2. Open terminal in the project folder
  3. Install dependencies:
       npm install
  4. Start development server:
       npm run dev
  5. Open browser at http://localhost:5173

To build for production:
       npm run build
  Output will be in the /dist folder.

To preview the production build:
       npm run preview


4. MICROSERVICE API ENDPOINTS USED
------------------------------------
All API calls are made to the Part 1 PHP backend on webdev.aut.ac.nz.

POST https://webdev.aut.ac.nz/~hpr7339/assign/booking.php
  Purpose  : Submit a new taxi booking
  Request  : FormData (cname, phone, unumber, snumber, stname, sbname, dsbname, date, time)
  Response : JSON { success, brn, time, date } on success
             JSON { success: false, message } on error

POST https://webdev.aut.ac.nz/~hpr7339/assign/admin.php
  action=search, bsearch=<BRN or empty>
  Purpose  : Search for a booking by BRN, or get all unassigned bookings within 2 hours
  Response : JSON { success, records: [...] }

  action=assign, brn=<BRN>
  Purpose  : Update booking status from "unassigned" to "assigned"
  Response : JSON { success, message }


5. FEATURE DESCRIPTIONS
------------------------

Feature 1: Map-Based Booking (BookingMap.jsx)
  An interactive SVG map of Auckland allows customers to click suburb pins
  to select their pickup and destination suburbs. The form pre-fills the
  suburb fields automatically. Customers complete their booking details and
  submit via the Part 1 booking.php API. A confirmation message with the
  booking reference number is displayed on successful submission.

Feature 2: Driver Querying Service (DriverService.jsx)
  Displays the full CabsOnline driver fleet with real-time status indicators
  (Available, On Trip, Offline). Admin staff can search or filter drivers by
  name, ID, or area. Selecting an available driver and entering a booking
  reference number assigns the driver to that booking. Driver profiles show
  vehicle details, current area, rating, and total trips completed.

Feature 3: Customer Monitoring Service (CustomerMonitor.jsx)
  Customers enter their booking reference number to track the status of their
  booking in real time. The component polls the admin.php API every 15 seconds
  and displays a visual timeline showing the progression through:
  Submitted → Pending → Assigned → En Route → Complete.
  An estimated arrival time is shown once the booking is assigned.
  A live stats dashboard shows total bookings, assigned, and pending counts.

Feature 4: Payment Processing (Payment.jsx)
  A fare calculator estimates the trip cost based on the selected pickup and
  destination suburbs using Auckland distance data, a base fare, per-km rate,
  and GST (15%). Customers enter card details through a styled payment form
  with real-time card number and expiry formatting. Payment processing is
  simulated (no real charges are made). A receipt is displayed on success.


6. TESTING INSTRUCTIONS
------------------------

Example Booking References (created during Part 1 testing):
  BRN00001
  BRN00002
  BRN00003

Sample Driver IDs (Feature 2):
  DRV001 – Mike Johnson    (Available, Auckland CBD)
  DRV002 – Sarah Lee       (On Trip, Ponsonby)
  DRV003 – James Patel     (Available, Newmarket)
  DRV005 – Tom Williams    (Available, Henderson)
  DRV007 – David Kim       (Available, Manukau)

Testing Feature 1 (Map Booking):
  1. Click "Select Pickup" then click a suburb pin on the map
  2. Click "Select Destination" then click a different suburb pin
  3. Fill in name, phone (10-12 digits), street number, street name
  4. Confirm date/time is not in the past
  5. Click "Confirm Booking →"
  6. Verify BRN confirmation message appears

Testing Feature 2 (Driver Service):
  1. Filter by "Available" to see only available drivers
  2. Click "Select" on any available driver (DRV001, DRV003, DRV005, DRV007)
  3. Enter a BRN from the example list above (e.g. BRN00001)
  4. Click "Assign Driver →"
  5. Verify success message appears

Testing Feature 3 (Customer Monitor):
  1. Enter a booking reference (e.g. BRN00001) in the search field
  2. Click "Track" or press Enter
  3. Verify the status timeline and booking details appear
  4. Observe the 15-second live refresh countdown

Testing Feature 4 (Payment):
  1. Select a pickup suburb and destination suburb in the fare calculator
  2. Observe the fare breakdown update automatically
  3. Enter a booking reference (e.g. BRN00001)
  4. Fill in test card details:
       Card Number : 4111 1111 1111 1111
       Name        : Test User
       Expiry      : 12/27
       CVV         : 123
  5. Click "Pay NZD $X.XX →"
  6. Observe loading animation, then success receipt


7. LIMITATIONS AND KNOWN ISSUES
---------------------------------
- The map in Feature 1 is a custom SVG representation of Auckland suburbs.
  It does not use live GPS or real map tiles. For a production system,
  Google Maps or Leaflet.js with OpenStreetMap tiles would be integrated.
- Driver status in Feature 2 uses mock data. In production, driver locations
  and statuses would be updated via a real-time database or WebSocket service.
- Payment processing in Feature 4 is fully simulated. No real payment gateway
  (e.g. Stripe, PayPal) is connected. Cards are not validated against a bank.
- The monitoring poll interval (15 seconds) is suitable for demonstration.
  A production system would use WebSockets for true real-time updates.
- CORS restrictions may affect direct API calls from StackBlitz to the AUT
  webdev server. If API calls fail, the app gracefully displays error messages.


8. REFLECTION ON AI-SUPPORTED DEVELOPMENT PROCESS
---------------------------------------------------
This project was developed with the assistance of Claude (Anthropic) as the
primary AI tool throughout the development process.

How AI was used:
  - Architecture planning: Claude helped design the four-feature component
    structure and the data flow between React components and the PHP API.
  - Code generation: All React components (BookingMap, DriverService,
    CustomerMonitor, Payment) were generated with Claude's assistance,
    based on the assignment specification requirements.
  - Debugging: Claude assisted in identifying and fixing issues such as
    date format conversions (YYYY-MM-DD vs DD/MM/YYYY), form validation
    logic, and CSS layout problems.
  - Documentation: This README was structured and written with Claude's help
    to ensure all required sections were covered thoroughly.

Reflection:
  AI-assisted development significantly accelerated the implementation of
  all four features within a single session. The most valuable aspect was
  Claude's ability to understand the assignment requirements and generate
  code that directly addressed the marking criteria. However, it was important
  to review every generated file to ensure correctness, especially for
  PHP-React integration and the specific name attributes required by the spec.
  AI is a powerful tool for scaffolding, but understanding and reviewing the
  output remains essential for producing quality, correct software.

